window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][103] = "<h5>EX-C33-10 · A mock loop day</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> With partners, run a four-round mock loop (a product round, a metrics round, a behavioral round and a &quot;why us and your questions&quot; round), each 45 minutes with 15-minute breaks. Score each round with the feedback checklist and note what changed as the day went on.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Self-scored; record if possible.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Use your own stories and briefs.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> About four hours.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The loop-day architecture in section 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> A good performance: each round starts with a plan stated aloud and a time check at the halfway point; evidence is quotable; answers recover openly when challenged; the last round is as sharp as the first because of the break routine (stand, water, one line on what to improve). Common findings: energy and structure fade in rounds three and four, questions at the end are rushed, and the &quot;why us&quot; is generic by the last round. Fix one thing per mock, such as the break routine or a prepared opener for the later rounds, and rerun.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Run two rounds back to back to rehearse transitions.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A long mock is tiring and the most realistic practice of stamina.</p>\n<span id=\"c33-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for four weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: format and allocation.</em> Use IQ-C33-02, EX-C33-01, EX-C33-04 and the loop map for a real or target company. Write the lens card (FW-C33-04) for two companies.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: briefs and evidence.</em> Use IQ-C33-01, IQ-C33-09, EX-C33-02, EX-C33-03, EX-C33-06 and EX-C33-08. Build two company briefs and record two &quot;why us&quot; answers.</li>\n<li><em>Week 3: Apple-lens and non-standard rounds.</em> Use IQ-C33-03 to IQ-C33-08, IQ-C33-10, EX-C33-05 and EX-C33-09. Answer each Apple-lens question aloud in about five minutes and rework the one you liked least.</li>\n<li><em>Week 4: full days and logistics.</em> Use IQ-C33-11, EX-C33-07 and EX-C33-10. Run a full mock loop day and a logistics rehearsal on the real device and network.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to take notes as an interviewer would, with evidence statements, and read them back; to interrupt with &quot;We're running short&quot; and &quot;Use this tool&quot;; and to ask &quot;any questions for me?&quot; at the end of every answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Re-score weekly.</strong> After each mock, rate your strength by round type from one to five and move hours (FW-C33-08).</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I confirm the format and the policy on assistance before preparing?</li>\n<li>Did I separate what is published from what is only reported?</li>\n<li>Did I allocate hours by importance times gap and re-score?</li>\n<li>Did my &quot;why us&quot; contain a product observation, a link to me and the team?</li>\n<li>Was my evidence easy to write down (a user, a number, a reason, a risk)?</li>\n<li>Did I state a time plan and check the clock?</li>\n<li>Did I ask two or three tailored questions and use an answer?</li>\n<li>Did I handle non-standard rounds with a time budget and an answer first?</li>\n<li>Did logistics work (tech check, backup, time zone, date)?</li>\n<li>Did I avoid breaking any policy, and avoid disclosing confidential details?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> For each drill record the date, the round type, the plan you stated, the evidence sentence you were proud of, the moment you lost time, your score out of ten and the one fix.</p>\n<span id=\"c33-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Use the next three weeks to prepare for the loop you are most likely to face.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> For each target company, write the lens card (FW-C33-04): what is published, what is only reported, the decision model, the round mix and the unknowns. Check each company's careers page for open windows and deadlines now, since early-career programs run fixed windows.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Ask each recruiter (once you have one) the format questions: stages, round types, durations, tools, decider, timeline and policy on assistance; update the loop maps. Rate your strength by round type after one mock each, and compute hours.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Build the company brief for your first loop (90 minutes), including your angle and three questions, and write the 90-second &quot;why us.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Run timed practice in the formats, with a partner taking notes as an interviewer. Rewrite weak answers so that evidence is quotable. Prepare tailored questions for each interviewer.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Practice one take-home or written exercise with a time budget, and one presentation to time.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 3.</strong> Run a full mock loop day with breaks, and a logistics rehearsal. Taper: stop new material two days before; light review of story headlines and the brief.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become assets:</strong> the lens cards and loop maps, the company briefs, the preparation plan with weekly re-scores, the &quot;why us&quot; answers, and the logs of what you were asked. Keep every claim about a company labeled as published or reported, and keep it current.</p>\n<span id=\"c33-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c33-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Know the loop: the stages, the round types, who decides (committee, hiring manager, panel, outside interviewer), the timeline and the policy on assistance, confirmed with the recruiter, and separate what a company publishes from what is only reported.</li>\n<li>Interviewers write evidence and decisions come from it: say your decision, your numbers and your reasons in quotable sentences, signpost your steps, recover openly from challenges and give the rubric-relevant parts.</li>\n<li>Allocate preparation by importance times gap for each round type, re-score weekly with timed mocks, prepare shared material once when you have several loops, and keep company-specific work separate.</li>\n<li>Build a one-page company brief (products, strategy, metrics, competitors, recent moves, your angle, three questions) from primary sources and use it to answer &quot;why us&quot; with a product observation, a link to your experience and a view, never with flattery.</li>\n<li>Handle non-standard rounds, questions to the interviewer and logistics deliberately: time-box take-homes and answer first, ask two or three tailored questions, test tools and time zones in advance, and follow the stated rules on outside help.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c33-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The careers pages and published culture or values documents of each company you are targeting (for example Amazon's Leadership Principles, Google's description of its hiring attributes, Netflix's culture document), read directly and with the date in mind <code>[V: company pages named, checked as sources in October 2026]</code>.</li>\n<li>Adam Bryant, &quot;In Head-Hunting, Big Data May Not Be Such a Big Deal,&quot; The New York Times (19 June 2013), the interview with Laszlo Bock; and Google re:Work's guides on structured interviewing and hiring by committee (rework.withgoogle.com). Laszlo Bock, <em>Work Rules!</em> (2015), is the book on Google's people practices (not read for this case). The sources behind CS-C33-02.</li>\n<li>Frank Schmidt and John Hunter, &quot;The Validity and Utility of Selection Methods in Personnel Psychology: Practical and Theoretical Implications of 85 Years of Research Findings,&quot; <em>Psychological Bulletin</em> 124(2), 262 to 274 (1998), on the predictive value of structured interviews and work samples. Later re-analyses (for example by Sackett and colleagues) report lower validities for several methods, including work samples, so treat the 1998 figures as upper estimates <code>[V: Psychological Bulletin 124(2), 262 to 274 (1998); the Sackett re-analysis figures come from a secondary summary]</code>.</li>\n<li>Gayle Laakmann McDowell and Jackie Bavaro, <em>Cracking the PM Interview</em> (2013), for an overview of PM interview formats, read with the date in mind <code>[V: CareerCup, first edition, 2 December 2013, ISBN 9780984782819]</code>.</li>\n<li>Amazon's 2017 letter to shareholders, on narrative memos, from Amazon's own site <code>[V: Amazon 2017 shareholder letter]</code>.</li>\n<li>A company's own recruiter materials, which are the best source on the current format of your specific loop.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c34\"></span>\n<h2>C34 — Mock Interviews, Scoring and Calibration</h2>\n<span id=\"c34-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>&quot;Tell me about a time you received critical feedback.&quot; &quot;You did well in practice and froze in the real interview; why?&quot; &quot;How do you know when you are ready?&quot; Interviewers rarely ask about your preparation directly, and yet the quality of your preparation shows in every round: in how you structure, how you recover, how well you know your own weaknesses. This chapter is the practice system for the whole book. Every earlier chapter gave you methods, model answers and exercises; this one tells you how to turn them into performance: how to run mock interviews alone, with a partner or recorded; how to score yourself honestly against the bands; how to find your weakest competency; how to practice it deliberately; how to manage nerves; how to use an AI tool without fooling yourself; and how to spend the last week.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code>: the practice system is not a round, and it determines performance in all of them. On the job, the same habits (record, score, find the one fix, test again) are how PMs improve their writing, presentations and decisions.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored, and what matters most.</strong> Whether your performance under realistic conditions is at the Strong band or above across round types, consistently, with stories that survive probing <code>Inferred</code>. The research on practice supports a simple rule that this chapter applies throughout: <em>retrieval and feedback beat rereading and hours</em>. CS-C34-02 reviews the evidence and its limits.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You practice under realistic conditions: timed, aloud, in the real format.</li>\n<li>You score every mock with a written scorecard and evidence, before anyone tells you the score.</li>\n<li>You check your self-scores against an outside judge and correct your bias.</li>\n<li>You keep an error log, pick one fix per session and retest within a few days.</li>\n<li>You find your weakest important competency from data, not from feeling, and aim practice at it.</li>\n<li>You manage nerves with preparation and recovery moves, use AI tools with a checking routine, and taper before a loop.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns end preparation: rereading chapters and calling it practice, hours logged with no feedback, inflated self-scores, practicing the strongest round type, a bad round that spirals into the next, and an AI tool that says every answer is great.</p>\n<span id=\"c34-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c34-c34-1-mock-system-solo-partner-recorded\"></span>\n<h4>C34.1 Mock system: solo, partner, recorded</h4>\n<p>A <strong>mock interview</strong> is a practice interview run under realistic conditions (the real format, the real time limit, no pausing or hints) and scored afterward against a rubric. It is retrieval practice for interviews: you must produce the answer, not recognize it (FW-C34-01).</p>\n<p><strong>Three formats.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Format</th>\n<th>How</th>\n<th>Strengths</th>\n<th>Weaknesses</th>\n<th>Best for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Solo, timed</td>\n<td>Prompt from the bank, timer, speak aloud (record audio), score yourself</td>\n<td>Anytime, free, many repetitions</td>\n<td>No interruptions, no pressure, easy to be lenient</td>\n<td>Drills; structure; timing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Partner (live)</td>\n<td>A peer or mentor plays the interviewer, asks probes, interrupts, scores independently</td>\n<td>Real pressure and probing; outside scoring</td>\n<td>Needs scheduling; partner skill varies</td>\n<td>Full rounds; probing; calibration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recorded (video or audio)</td>\n<td>Record any mock; review with the rubric and transcript</td>\n<td>Shows filler, pace, structure, body language</td>\n<td>Takes time to review</td>\n<td>Delivery; self-scoring evidence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Cadence.</strong> A reasonable plan for a twelve-week preparation: weeks one to four, three solo drills a week and one partner mock; weeks five to eight, two partner mocks a week and a recorded review of each; weeks nine to twelve, a full mock loop every two weeks and targeted drills in between; a taper in the final week (C34.10). Adjust to the hours you have (C33.14).</p>\n<p><strong>Setting up a partner mock.</strong> Agree the round type and length; give the partner the rubric and the prompt (not your preferred answer); ask them to take notes as evidence statements (C33.2); ask them to probe three levels deep and to interrupt once; ask for scores before discussion; then debrief for ten minutes, ending with one fix. Swap roles: scoring other people's answers is itself some of the best practice, because you learn what evidence looks like from the interviewer's side.</p>\n<p><strong>Finding partners.</strong> Peers preparing for the same roles, alumni and colleagues in product roles, mentors, and structured communities; offer to be the interviewer for them. Rotate partners so that you are not tuned to one person's style.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> A candidate logs 100 hours over two months, mostly rereading chapters and watching sample interviews, with a few unrecorded solo answers that she scores 4 out of 5 herself. In her first partner mock the partner scores her 2.5 on metrics and 3 overall, and she runs out of time before reaching the metrics. The hours were real and mostly unmeasured, and the confidence was unearned. A better start: a timed partner mock in the first week, scored independently, with one fix and a retest.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Realism rules.</strong> Same time limit; no stopping to think for minutes (a short pause is fine); no notes if the real interview forbids them; the same tool (document or whiteboard) you will use; dress and setting as in the real interview at least once.</p>\n<span id=\"c34-c34-2-rubrics-by-round-type\"></span>\n<h4>C34.2 Rubrics by round type</h4>\n<p>A <strong>scorecard</strong> is a one-page form with the dimensions of a round, a score for each anchored to the bands, the evidence for each score and an overall lean (hire, lean hire, lean no hire, no hire) (FW-C34-02). The scorecards below are the author's synthesis from the interview-bank rubrics in this book, from the attributes companies publish and from the evidence-based practice described in C33.2; companies do not publish scoring rubrics at this resolution <code>Original</code> <code>Inferred</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Scale.</strong> Five points, anchored: <strong>1</strong> not shown or harmful; <strong>2</strong> Baseline (vocabulary without substance); <strong>3</strong> adequate but generic; <strong>4</strong> Strong (clear hire signal on this dimension); <strong>5</strong> Standout (a reason to hire). Scores of 4 and above across most dimensions, with no 1 or 2 on a dimension central to the round, is what a competitive loop looks like <code>Inferred</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Product sense, design and improvement (C06 to C11).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>Baseline (2)</th>\n<th>Strong (4)</th>\n<th>Standout (5)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Framing</td>\n<td>Restates the prompt; jumps to features</td>\n<td>Clarifies goal and context; states the problem for a chosen user</td>\n<td>Reframes the problem in a way that changes the options; names assumptions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>User empathy</td>\n<td>Generic &quot;users&quot;</td>\n<td>Distinct segments, a chosen one, real needs with evidence</td>\n<td>A specific scene from the user's day; the non-obvious need</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Solutions</td>\n<td>A list of features</td>\n<td>Several distinct ideas, one chosen with reasons</td>\n<td>A bold option plus a safe one; tradeoffs priced</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metrics and risks</td>\n<td>Names a metric</td>\n<td>Primary metric with guardrails; risks</td>\n<td>A decision rule; second-order effects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Communication</td>\n<td>Rambling</td>\n<td>Structured, signposted, time-managed</td>\n<td>Crisp; adapts to interruptions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Analytical, metrics and execution (C05, C12 to C15, C17).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>Baseline (2)</th>\n<th>Strong (4)</th>\n<th>Standout (5)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Structure</td>\n<td>Lists causes</td>\n<td>A tree or hypothesis list, prioritized</td>\n<td>Falsifiable hypotheses with the first cheap check</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metric choice</td>\n<td>A vanity metric</td>\n<td>Defined with numerator, denominator and window; guardrails</td>\n<td>Anticipates gaming; ties to the decision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quantitative reasoning</td>\n<td>Hand-waving</td>\n<td>Correct arithmetic; sanity checks</td>\n<td>Uses uncertainty and ranges; finds the segment insight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Experiment and causality</td>\n<td>&quot;Run an A/B test&quot;</td>\n<td>Unit, duration, power, pitfalls</td>\n<td>Handles novelty, interference and long-term effects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recommendation</td>\n<td>Describes data</td>\n<td>Decides with a rule and a risk</td>\n<td>States what would change the decision</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Estimation (C04).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>Baseline (2)</th>\n<th>Strong (4)</th>\n<th>Standout (5)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Structure</td>\n<td>A single guess</td>\n<td>A decomposition with stated assumptions</td>\n<td>Two independent approaches that cross-check</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Arithmetic</td>\n<td>Errors</td>\n<td>Correct, rounded sensibly</td>\n<td>Clean order-of-magnitude handling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sanity check</td>\n<td>None</td>\n<td>Compares with a known figure</td>\n<td>Explains the likely error and its direction</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Technical and system design (C21, C22).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>Baseline (2)</th>\n<th>Strong (4)</th>\n<th>Standout (5)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fluency</td>\n<td>Uses terms loosely</td>\n<td>Explains how it works in plain words</td>\n<td>Chooses the right level for the audience</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>System thinking</td>\n<td>Boxes with no flow</td>\n<td>Components, data flow, failure modes</td>\n<td>Latency, scale and cost numbers; degradation design</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tradeoffs</td>\n<td>Lists options</td>\n<td>Chooses with a reason</td>\n<td>States what would change the choice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Questions</td>\n<td>Few</td>\n<td>Clarifies constraints</td>\n<td>Questions that reveal a hidden requirement</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>AI and ML (C23 to C25).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>Baseline (2)</th>\n<th>Strong (4)</th>\n<th>Standout (5)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fit</td>\n<td>&quot;Use AI&quot;</td>\n<td>States the decision, error cost and a rule-based alternative</td>\n<td>Applies a fit test and tolerable error rate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Failure modes</td>\n<td>Mentions hallucination</td>\n<td>Maps failure modes to mitigations</td>\n<td>Layers, red-team, upper bounds, kill switch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation</td>\n<td>&quot;Test it&quot;</td>\n<td>Test set, rubric, human review</td>\n<td>Sample size, agreement, calibrated model grader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cost and risk</td>\n<td>Ignored</td>\n<td>A cost per request estimate</td>\n<td>Margin by segment; regulation by risk tier</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Behavioral and communication (C31, C32).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>Baseline (2)</th>\n<th>Strong (4)</th>\n<th>Standout (5)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ownership and decision</td>\n<td>&quot;We&quot; throughout; no decision</td>\n<td>States the decision and options; &quot;I&quot; for own actions</td>\n<td>Owns a hard call and its cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evidence</td>\n<td>No numbers</td>\n<td>Numbers with baselines</td>\n<td>Honest limits; credit for others</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Probing</td>\n<td>Story collapses</td>\n<td>Three levels of concrete answers</td>\n<td>Admits flaws; shows judgment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Learning</td>\n<td>Generic</td>\n<td>A changed behavior</td>\n<td>Evidence the new habit works</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Structure and delivery</td>\n<td>Rambling</td>\n<td>Answer first; 2 to 3 minutes</td>\n<td>Crisp; adapts to the listener</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Using a scorecard.</strong> Score each dimension from evidence, not impression: write the sentence you said or the thing you did next to the score. Then set the overall lean from the pattern.</p>\n<span id=\"c34-c34-3-three-full-mock-loops\"></span>\n<h4>C34.3 Three full mock loops</h4>\n<p>Section 5 contains three annotated mock loops, all <code>Illustrative</code> with invented content: a Google-style loop (product design, analytical and behavioral rounds), an Amazon-style loop (principle-based behavioral rounds with deep probing and a written exercise) and an Apple-style loop (team-specific, craft-focused, with a final panel). Each shows condensed transcripts, the evidence an interviewer would write, scorecards and a debrief. They are not scripts to imitate. Read them with the scorecards in C34.2 open, score each round yourself first and then compare.</p>\n<span id=\"c34-c34-4-self-scoring-and-calibration-against-the-bands\"></span>\n<h4>C34.4 Self-scoring and calibration against the bands</h4>\n<p><strong>Calibration</strong> is the alignment of your self-assessment with outside judgment: how closely your scores match those of an independent, competent judge (FW-C34-03). Without it you may practice the wrong things with confidence. The research behind this chapter is a warning: in a well-known experiment, students who repeatedly reread material felt much more confident than students who tested themselves, and remembered less a week later (CS-C34-02). Feeling fluent is not evidence of being good.</p>\n<p><strong>Method.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>After a mock, and before any feedback, write your score on each dimension with the evidence.</li>\n<li>Have the partner score independently with the same scorecard.</li>\n<li>Compare. For each dimension compute the difference (yours minus theirs). Over eight mocks, compute your <em>bias</em> (the mean difference: positive means you overrate yourself) and your <em>error</em> (the mean absolute difference).</li>\n<li>Look at where you differ by two or more points and talk it through: who had the better evidence?</li>\n<li>Adjust: if your bias is +0.75, subtract about three-quarters of a point from your self-scores until it shrinks, and use the partner's scores for decisions.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Worked example 4 computes a bias of +0.75 over eight mocks.</p>\n<p><strong>Anchors help.</strong> Use the written anchors (C34.2) and your own saved examples of 2s, 4s and 5s so that your scale does not drift. Re-read the anchors before scoring, and score recordings you made a month ago to check your consistency. Where two scorers agree beyond chance the scale is working; where they do not, the anchors need examples (the same lesson as C25.1 on rater agreement).</p>\n<span id=\"c34-c34-5-the-answer-journal-review-loop\"></span>\n<h4>C34.5 The answer-journal review loop</h4>\n<p>An <strong>error log</strong> is a running list of the specific mistakes you made in practice, each with its cause and the fix you will apply, which you review regularly (FW-C34-04). The loop:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Do</strong> a mock or drill under realistic conditions.</li>\n<li><strong>Record</strong> the prompt, the time taken, what you said (the recording or notes) and your scorecard.</li>\n<li><strong>Score</strong> with evidence, before the partner's feedback.</li>\n<li><strong>Find one fix.</strong> From the scorecard and the partner's notes, choose the single change that would raise the score most (for example, &quot;state the metric's numerator and denominator&quot;).</li>\n<li><strong>Log</strong> the error: what happened, the cause, the fix. Entries are specific (&quot;spent 14 minutes on users, left 3 for solutions&quot;), not generic (&quot;manage time better&quot;).</li>\n<li><strong>Drill</strong> the fix in isolation (C34.6) for ten to fifteen minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Retest within about 72 hours</strong> on a different prompt of the same type; then again after a week. Spacing the retest rather than repeating it at once is a separate, widely used practice (the spacing effect); the testing effect itself, in CS-C34-02, is about retrieving rather than rereading.</li>\n<li><strong>Review the log weekly:</strong> which errors recur? Recurring errors become the next week's drills.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What to record each time.</strong> Date, round type, prompt, minutes used by phase, score by dimension (self and partner), the one fix, the retest date. Over weeks the log is the data for diagnosing your weakest competency (C34.7).</p>\n<span id=\"c34-c34-6-drill-library-by-question-type\"></span>\n<h4>C34.6 Drill library by question type</h4>\n<p>A drill is a short, focused exercise on one skill, ten to fifteen minutes, with a clear success test (FW-C34-05). Use drills for the fixes the log reveals; use full mocks to check that the skill holds together.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Drill</th>\n<th>How to run it</th>\n<th>Success test</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Answer-first in 60 seconds</td>\n<td>Take any prompt; give the answer and three supports in 60 seconds; record</td>\n<td>The answer in the first 15 seconds; three named supports</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clarifying questions</td>\n<td>For ten prompts, write the three questions that most change the answer</td>\n<td>Each question would change the design or the metric</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Structure on a blank page</td>\n<td>Build an issue tree or a user-problem map in 3 minutes</td>\n<td>MECE branches; a first check named</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metric definition</td>\n<td>Define a metric with numerator, denominator, window, guardrail in 2 minutes</td>\n<td>No missing element; a gaming risk named</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Estimation</td>\n<td>One estimation in 8 minutes, two approaches</td>\n<td>Answers within a factor of 2 of each other; a sanity check</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diagnose a metric change</td>\n<td>A prompt with a 5% drop: list hypotheses and the order of checks in 5 minutes</td>\n<td>Falsifiable hypotheses; cheapest check first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Story compression</td>\n<td>Tell a story in 3 minutes, then in 90 seconds, then in 30</td>\n<td>Decision and result survive each cut</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Probe ladder</td>\n<td>A partner asks six probes (three levels) on a story</td>\n<td>No invented detail; flaws admitted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Technical explanation</td>\n<td>Explain an API or a cache to a non-technical person in 90 seconds</td>\n<td>The listener can repeat it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AI failure-mode map</td>\n<td>Take an AI feature; fill the failure-mode map in 8 minutes</td>\n<td>Each failure has a mitigation and a test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The no script</td>\n<td>Write and say three no scripts in 5 minutes</td>\n<td>Goal acknowledged; alternative and path to yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recovery moves</td>\n<td>Practice the three recovery phrases (C34.8) aloud</td>\n<td>Said calmly, within 5 seconds of noticing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>When not to drill.</strong> If the problem is a gap in knowledge (you do not know what a numerator and denominator are, or how a cache works), learn it first from the relevant chapter, and drill afterward; drilling a skill you have not understood reinforces errors. Do not drill a dimension that already scores 4 consistently; spend the time on a weaker one.</p>\n<p><strong>Rules.</strong> One skill per drill; record or write; check against the success test; log the result; repeat on a new prompt in 72 hours.</p>\n<span id=\"c34-c34-7-diagnosing-the-weakest-competency-and-targeted-practic\"></span>\n<h4>C34.7 Diagnosing the weakest competency and targeted practice</h4>\n<p>After six to eight scored mocks you have data (FW-C34-06). Do not rely on feeling.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Tabulate</strong> scores by dimension across mocks (use the partner's or the calibrated scores).</li>\n<li><strong>Find the lowest average</strong> and the highest variance. A low, stable score is a skill gap (train it). A high variance is a consistency gap (add a checklist or a drill that makes it routine).</li>\n<li><strong>Weight by stakes.</strong> A weak dimension that appears in many rounds or that carries a veto matters more (importance × gap, C33.14).</li>\n<li><strong>Pick one or two targets</strong> and set a number: &quot;metrics dimension from 2.3 to 3.5 in three weeks.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Practice deliberately.</strong> <strong>Deliberate practice</strong> is a form of practice, described in the research on expertise, in which you work on a specific aspect of performance, with immediate informative feedback and repeated attempts at the same or similar tasks <code>[V: Ericsson, Krampe and Tesch-Römer, Psychological Review (1993)]</code>. In this book: a drill, a scored retest and a fix.</li>\n<li><strong>Re-score</strong> after two weeks and move to the next weakest dimension. Stop adding hours to a dimension that has reached 4 and a consistent pattern.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Worked example 5 diagnoses a candidate whose metrics dimension averages 2.3 and allocates 20 hours.</p>\n<span id=\"c34-c34-8-managing-nerves-and-recovering-from-a-bad-round\"></span>\n<h4>C34.8 Managing nerves and recovering from a bad round</h4>\n<p>Some nervousness is normal and can sharpen attention; the aim is to keep it from damaging structure and judgment. What helps, in order of reliability <code>Inferred</code>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Preparation under realistic conditions.</strong> The more your mocks resemble the real round, the less new the real round feels. This is the main reason to run timed partner mocks.</li>\n<li><strong>A routine.</strong> The same opening each time: a breath, the plan stated aloud (&quot;I'll clarify, structure, answer, then check risks&quot;), the first clarifying question. A routine gives your mind a track to run on.</li>\n<li><strong>Pacing.</strong> Nerves speed speech. Slow the first sentence deliberately; pause before answering; sentences are shorter and clearer.</li>\n<li><strong>Body basics.</strong> Sleep, food, water, a short walk before; slow breathing with a longer exhale for a minute before a round (a short adaptation of practices studied at five minutes a day) <code>[V: Balban et al., Cell Reports Medicine 4(1), 2023: daily 5-minute exhale-focused breathing improved mood and lowered respiratory rate more than mindfulness; one minute before a round is an adaptation; sleep, food, water and a walk are general advice, not sourced]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Reframing.</strong> Treat the interview as a conversation to collect evidence of how you think, not a verdict on you. A question you cannot answer is a chance to show how you think when you cannot.</li>\n<li><strong>If anxiety is severe or persistent,</strong> talk to a doctor or a counselor; this is outside what a book can address.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Recovery moves in the round (FW-C34-07).</strong> Practice three phrases until they are automatic. <em>When you blank:</em> &quot;Let me take a moment to structure this.&quot; Then structure. <em>When you notice a mistake:</em> &quot;I want to correct something I said: it should be X.&quot; Then continue. <em>When you are lost in the middle:</em> &quot;Let me summarize where I am and what I still need to cover.&quot; Then state the plan. Interviewers record how you recover; a clean recovery is a positive data point.</p>\n<p><strong>Between rounds.</strong> Stand, drink water, write one line about what to improve, and leave the last round behind. Do not replay it. A bad round has a limited effect on a loop that has other rounds, and there is no way to know how it was scored.</p>\n<p><strong>After a bad loop.</strong> Write down the questions and your answers within an hour; note what you would change; give yourself a day; then go back to the log and fix the one or two errors that mattered. A rejection is information about one loop at one time (the base rate of rejection at selective companies is high <code>Inferred</code>); it is not a verdict on your ability.</p>\n<span id=\"c34-c34-9-using-ai-as-a-practice-partner-strengths-blind-spots-a\"></span>\n<h4>C34.9 Using AI as a practice partner: strengths, blind spots and a checking routine <code>[as of 2026-10]</code></h4>\n<p>AI tools can speed practice and can mislead it. Used well they are a cheap interviewer available at any hour (FW-C34-08).</p>\n<p><strong>Strengths.</strong> Generating prompts for a round type; playing an interviewer who asks probes; summarizing a transcript of your answer and listing where the structure broke; comparing your answer to a rubric you supply; suggesting follow-up questions; producing a company reading list.</p>\n<p><strong>Blind spots.</strong> <em>Flattery and lenient scoring</em> (models tend to agree with the user and rate answers highly); <em>inconsistency</em> (the same answer can get different scores); <em>invented facts</em> about companies, products and processes (hallucination, C24.3); <em>no real pressure or real interruption</em>; <em>a preference for fluent, long answers</em> that real interviewers penalize; and a reluctance to say a hard thing plainly.</p>\n<p><strong>A checking routine.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Give the rubric</strong> (the scorecard from C34.2) and ask for scores with the sentences from your answer as evidence for each. If it cannot quote you, discard the score.</li>\n<li><strong>Ask for the strongest criticism</strong> first, and for what a skeptical interviewer would write.</li>\n<li><strong>Run it twice</strong> on the same answer and compare; large differences mean the score is noise.</li>\n<li><strong>Calibrate it</strong> against a human: score five of your answers with a partner and with the tool; compute the tool's bias and error as in C34.4. If it overrates by a point, subtract a point, or use it only for ideas, not scores (the same discipline as C25.1 on model graders).</li>\n<li><strong>Verify every fact</strong> about a company or a process against primary sources (C33.10); treat the tool's claims as hypotheses.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep real practice real:</strong> use the tool for prompts and critiques, but do live mocks with a person at least weekly.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not paste confidential material</strong> from a past or current employer.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not use an AI tool during a real interview</strong> unless the company explicitly permits it for that round (C33.13).</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c34-c34-10-the-final-week-protocol\"></span>\n<h4>C34.10 The final-week protocol</h4>\n<p>The last week is for consolidation and rest, not for new material (FW-C34-02 and the checklist below). A typical schedule for a loop on day 0:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Day</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>−7</td>\n<td>Last full mock loop; score it; choose the one or two fixes that matter most</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>−6</td>\n<td>Drill the fixes; update the error log; confirm logistics in writing (times, time zone, tools, policy)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>−5</td>\n<td>Review the company brief and your &quot;why us&quot;; rehearse your tailored stories aloud</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>−4</td>\n<td>Two short timed drills on the weakest dimension; no new frameworks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>−3</td>\n<td>A final partner mock of one round type (not a full loop)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>−2</td>\n<td>Light review: story headlines, brief, questions to ask; tech check on the real device and network; stop new material</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>−1</td>\n<td>Rest. A short walk, a light review of one page, early sleep; prepare clothes, water, notes; do not cram</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>Routine: wake early, food, light movement, 15-minute review of headlines, tech check, a breath before each round</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>After</td>\n<td>Notes within an hour; thank-you within a day; log the questions; rest</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Rules.</strong> No new methods in the last three days; sleep protected; logistics settled two days before; one last look at the policy on assistance; arrive with a plan for each round and a routine between them. If you are interviewing across time zones, apply the sleep and logistics advice from C33.13.</p>\n<span id=\"c34-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Eight frameworks, set out below. The scorer's view (FW-C33-02), the round-time budget (FW-C33-03), the company-specific preparation plan (FW-C33-08), the probe ladder (FW-C31-05) and the failure-story test (FW-C31-06) are reused, not repeated.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c34-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C34-01 · The mock plan · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a target date, weekly hours and your weakest round types.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a schedule of solo drills, partner mocks and recorded reviews; realism rules; partner roles and a debrief format; a cadence that rises toward full mock loops and tapers in the last week.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can find at least two partners and record yourself.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> more mocks give more feedback and cost more partner time; solo drills are cheap and lenient.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> only solo practice; one partner; no recording; mocks without scores.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 4 and EX-C34-01.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c34-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C34-02 · The scorecard · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a round type and its dimensions (C34.2).</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a one-page form: dimensions, a 1 to 5 score anchored to the bands, the evidence sentence for each score, and an overall lean (hire, lean hire, lean no hire, no hire).</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> scoring is done from evidence, before discussion, by you and an independent scorer.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a detailed form is slower to fill and more reliable.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> scores with no evidence; inflating to feel good; changing the scale between mocks.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked examples 1 to 3.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>Score (1 to 5)</th>\n<th>Evidence (what was said or done)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c34-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C34-03 · The calibration check · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> self-scores and an independent scorer's scores for at least six mocks.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> bias (mean of self minus other), error (mean absolute difference), the share of scores within one point, the dimensions where you differ by two or more, and a correction to apply to self-scores.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the outside scorer is competent and uses the same anchors.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> costs a scorer's time and removes the main source of false confidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> scoring after hearing the other's score; a lenient friend as the only scorer.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 4 and EX-C34-03.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Formulas: <strong>bias = mean(self − other); error = mean(|self − other|).</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c34-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C34-04 · The answer-journal review loop · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a mock or drill and its recording or notes.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> an error log entry (what happened, cause, one fix), a drill for the fix, a retest on a new prompt within about 72 hours and again after a week, and a weekly review of recurring errors.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you log specifics, not generalities.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> logging takes ten minutes a session and turns practice into improvement.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> vague entries; many fixes at once; no retest.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> CS-C34-01.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Date</th>\n<th>Round</th>\n<th>Error (specific)</th>\n<th>Cause</th>\n<th>One fix</th>\n<th>Drill</th>\n<th>Retest date and score</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c34-05\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C34-05 · The drill library entry · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a skill a fix targets.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a ten to fifteen minute drill with a run instruction and a success test, recorded in the library (C34.6), with the date last run and the result.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> one skill per drill.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> drills build skills in isolation; mocks check that they combine.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> drilling several skills at once; no success test.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> EX-C34-05.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c34-06\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C34-06 · The weakest-competency diagnosis · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> six to eight calibrated scorecards.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the average and variance by dimension, importance by round weight, importance × gap, one or two targets with numeric goals, hours allocated (importance × gap scaled to the weekly hours), and a re-score date.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> scores are calibrated.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a data-driven plan can neglect a strength that needs maintenance.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> practicing the favorite dimension; changing targets weekly; no numeric goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 5 and EX-C34-06.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c34-07\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C34-07 · The recovery moves and nerves plan · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> your three most likely in-round failures (blanking, an error, losing the thread).</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a rehearsed phrase and a next action for each, an opening routine, a pacing rule, between-round reset steps and an after-a-bad-round protocol.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you rehearse the phrases aloud.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a routine can feel mechanical; it frees attention for the question.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> improvising a recovery under pressure; replaying the last round.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> EX-C34-07 and IQ-C34-04.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Moment</th>\n<th>Phrase</th>\n<th>Next action</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Blank</td>\n<td>&quot;Let me take a moment to structure this.&quot;</td>\n<td>Write two or three headings; continue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mistake</td>\n<td>&quot;I want to correct something: it should be X.&quot;</td>\n<td>Fix and continue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lost thread</td>\n<td>&quot;Let me summarize where I am and what's left.&quot;</td>\n<td>State the plan and the remaining steps</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c34-08\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C34-08 · The AI practice-partner checking routine · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> an AI tool, the scorecard and your answers.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> scores with quoted evidence; the strongest criticism first; two runs compared; a calibration against a human (bias and error); verification of any fact against a primary source; and a rule for what the tool is used for (prompts and critique) and not (final scores, facts, live interviews unless permitted).</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the tool tends to flatter and to be inconsistent.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> cheap, fast feedback against reliability.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> accepting a high score; uncorrected bias; unchecked facts; pasting confidential material.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> EX-C34-08 and IQ-C34-06.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c34-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n";
